<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cathay</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khitay, Kitai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cathay is the medieval European name for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, derived from the Khitan, the people who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>. The name passed through Central Asian forms such as Khitay and was popularised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>; it survives in the Russian name for China, Kitai. For centuries Europeans thought of Cathay and China as separate countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cathay.jpg" alt="Cathay" width="320" height="274" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cresques Abraham · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empire_of_the_Great_Khan_(Catayo)_according_to_the_Catalan_Atlas_(1375).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
